.TH WHOAMI 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
whoami \- print the name of the current user
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whoami
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whoami
prints the name of the current user, followed by a newline, on standard
output.
It takes no operands.
This
.B whoami
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Windows has no Unix user ids, so busybox supplies fixed ones, and
.B whoami
prints the name of the effective user:
.IP \(bu 2
In a process that is elevated and has the Administrators group enabled,
such as one started by
.BR su (1),
the user is
.BR root .
.IP \(bu 2
In any other process the user is the Windows account, named as the
Windows API function
.B GetUserName
returns it.
The name has no domain, keeps the case that Windows gives it, and has
each space replaced by an underscore.
.PP
This is the same name that
.B "id \-un"
prints; see
.BR id (1).
The name does not come from the environment, so setting
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR USER ,
or
.B USERNAME
does not change it.
.SH OPTIONS
Apart from
.BR \-\-help ,
.B whoami
takes no options.
Any other argument, including
.BR \-\- ,
a lone
.BR \- ,
and
.BR \-\-version ,
is rejected with a usage message on standard error and exit status 1.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An argument other than a first
.BR \-\-help ,
or a failure to get the name.
.TP
.B 255
The output could not be written.
No message is printed.
.PP
A rejected argument is reported with a usage message on standard error.
A failure to get the name is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
whoami: unknown uid 4095
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the name of the current user:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ whoami
alice
.EE
.RE
.PP
For an account named
.BR "Jane Doe" ,
each space becomes an underscore:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ whoami
Jane_Doe
.EE
.RE
.PP
In an elevated process, such as one started with Run as administrator:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ whoami
root
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the Windows program, which prints the domain as well.
The shell runs the applet for a bare
.BR whoami ,
so name the program by its path:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ c:/windows/system32/whoami
.EE
.RE
.PP
Tell whether the process is elevated:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ [ "$(id \-u)" = 0 ] && echo elevated
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR whoami :
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-\-version ,
and
.B \-\-help
is recognized only as the first argument.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no user database to read.
The one user is the account that Windows reports for the process, or
.B root
when the process is elevated.
.PP
An elevated process is
.B root
whatever its Windows account is named.
An account that is really named
.B root
is therefore not told apart from an elevated process by its name, whereas
.B "id \-u"
prints 0 only when the process is elevated.
A command started by
.BR drop ,
.BR cdrop ,
or
.B pdrop
runs with reduced privileges and is not root, even from an elevated
process, so
.B whoami
prints the account name there.
.PP
Windows has its own
.BR whoami.exe ,
which prints the domain as well, as in
.IR domain \e account ,
and has options of its own.
The kit's shell runs
.B whoami
as an applet without consulting
.BR PATH ,
so it runs the busybox one, unless
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
says otherwise; see
.BR busybox (1).
It does so inside its own process, without starting another; see
.BR sh (1).
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
the order of directories in
.B PATH
decides which one runs.
.PP
The user name is read in the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10
version 1903 and later, so on those versions it may contain any Unicode
characters.
On older versions it is limited to the system code page.
A name of 100 bytes or more cannot be read, and fails as described in
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.PP
The output line ends with LF, not CRLF.
When standard output is a console, a name with non-ASCII characters is
converted for display from the ANSI code page, unless the variables in
.B ENVIRONMENT
say otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR id (1),
.BR logname (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR su (1)
